SUMMARY:Cambridge Legal Theory Discussion Group (CLTDG): 'Is Consent Fundam
 entally Irrelevant to Sex Crimes?'
LOCATION:Faculty of Law\, David Williams Building
URL:https://www.law.cam.ac.uk/events/cambridge-legal-theory-discussion-grou
 p-cltdg-consent-fundamentally-irrelevant-sex-crimes
CATEGORIES:Law,Criminology
DESCRIPTION:Faculty of Law\nTopics: Law\, Criminology\nDetails: https://www
 .law.cam.ac.uk/events/cambridge-legal-theory-discussion-group-cltdg-consen
 t-fundamentally-irrelevant-sex-crimes\nAdam Kolber (Brooklyn Law School)\n
 \n Date/time: \n\n Tuesday 1 December 2026 \n\n 6:15pm to 7:45pm\n\n Locat
 ion: \n\n Faculty of Law\, David Williams Building\n\n G24\n\n About \n\n 
 Abstract : Consent has famously been described as moral magic because of i
 ts purported power to transform otherwise wrongful sexual contact into per
 missible conduct. On this “transformative” view\, a person’s consent
  changes what others may permissibly do\, at least sometimes allowing sex 
 to occur even when it’s expected to make the consenter significantly wor
 se off. I defend an alternative. Under the “evidential” view\, consent
  is a proxy for a person’s welfare. It is our best indicator of how sexu
 al contact is likely to affect that person’s welfare. Nothing about the 
 evidential view diminishes consent’s importance to the law\, but it chan
 ges our understanding of what the law should be doing and may recommend ad
 justments at the
END:VEVENT
